Description: do not put a MsvAvFlags=0 pair into Type2
 Windows SSPI modifies a MsvAvFlags pair received in Type2 in place
 (|= MIC_PRESENT) and computes the MIC over its own, already modified copy of
 CHALLENGE_MESSAGE. The acceptor stores and signs Type2 as it sent it, so the
 client MIC never matched (DEFECTIVE_TOKEN). Without the pair in Type2 Windows
 appends the flags in a separate blob, and the MIC covers Type2 as sent on the
 wire, like spec-compliant clients. MIC verification is not weakened.
Author: Claude Code Server <noreply@anthropic.com>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-06

--- a/src/gss_sec_ctx.c	2023-02-07 19:31:20.447164538 +0300
+++ b/src/gss_sec_ctx.c	2026-10-06 16:46:28.208883365 +0300
@@ -751,12 +751,17 @@
 
         timestamp = ntlm_timestamp_now();
 
+        /* A MsvAvFlags=0 pair in Type2 is not needed and is harmful: Windows SSPI
         * edits it in place in its copy of CHALLENGE_MESSAGE (|= MIC_PRESENT) and
         * computes the MIC over the edited Type2, whereas without the pair it
         * appends the flags in a separate blob and the MIC covers the wire Type2. */
         retmin = ntlm_encode_target_info(ctx->ntlm,
                                          nb_computer_name,
                                          nb_domain_name,
                                          server_name->data.server.name,
                                          NULL, NULL,
-                                         &av_flags, &timestamp,
+                                         av_flags ? &av_flags : NULL,
+                                         &timestamp,
                                          NULL,
                                          server_name->data.server.spn,
                                          NULL,
